# === DRAFT ONLY — NOT ADDED TO ANY CRONTAB — awaiting explicit approval ===
#
# v3 — supersedes control_arm_crontab_v2.draft. Fixes applied after a
# second external audit found three remaining problems in v2:
#   1. --env-file /root/AI-Stock-Radar-Control/.env added to the command.
#      v2 relied on run_control_arm_decision.py's transitive imports
#      (src/data/alpaca_market_data.py, src/notify/telegram_notifier.py)
#      loading the repo-root .env normally at import time, with no
#      fail-closed check of that path at all -- the wrapper's own
#      fail-closed _load_env_file_fail_closed() function only ran when
#      --env-file was explicitly passed, which v2's cron entry never
#      did. Production and this session's own sandbox test were
#      therefore silently using two DIFFERENT credential-loading code
#      paths (implicit vs. explicit/fail-closed) -- the whole point of
#      building a fail-closed loader is defeated if production never
#      actually exercises it. Now both paths call the identical
#      function: Path(...).resolve(strict=True) (raises immediately if
#      the file is missing/unresolvable, never a silent fallback to
#      whatever else might already be in os.environ) + a check that
#      load_dotenv() actually reported loading something.
#   2. The BTC/universe leak check now runs in TWO layers, not one:
#      - NEW pre-flight check (_preflight_universe_check in
#        run_control_arm_decision.py), which reads the persisted state
#        file DIRECTLY and refuses to call run_daily_decision() AT ALL
#        -- zero API calls -- if it already contains any
#        position/pending-buy/pending-exit for BTC-USD or any ticker
#        outside the 44-ticker control universe. This is the layer that
#        actually matters for a STALE entry left behind by an earlier
#        run/bug/hand-edit -- v2's check ran only AFTER
#        run_daily_decision() had already fetched live data, written
#        new state, and (if enabled) could have submitted real orders,
#        so it could catch this run's OWN mistake but never a
#        pre-existing one.
#      - The v2 post-hoc audit check is KEPT, unchanged, as a second,
#        independent layer (if the pre-flight check somehow didn't fire,
#        this still would) -- not replaced, since a check that runs
#        after a real state-changing call is still worth having even
#        though it is provably weaker on its own.
#   3. --enable-crypto-orders' help text corrected: no longer claims
#      unconditional "no-op." BTC-USD can never generate a NEW entry
#      signal in this universe (RegimeAllowed force-set False), but if a
#      stale BTC-USD position/pending entry ever existed in state, this
#      flag's crypto order-submission logic (reused unmodified from
#      run_daily_decision.py) could still act on it -- item #2's new
#      pre-flight check is what actually prevents that scenario from
#      ever reaching this flag, not the flag's own semantics.
#
# (v2's fixes, unchanged, still in effect: real operational entry point
# -- scripts/run_control_arm_decision.py, not run_daily_decision.py
# directly; single-physical-line cron syntax; 22:02 UTC schedule, 47min
# after the live system's real 21:15 UTC run, not a multiple of 5 so it
# never lands in the same minute as the */5 crypto stop monitor.)
#
# DST — STILL AN OPEN RISK, STILL NOT RESOLVED: SSH to the production
# server is still blocked from this sandbox. A bare `H M * * 1-5` cron
# line runs at a fixed UTC instant regardless of US Eastern Time's DST
# transitions -- if the live system's 21:15 UTC is itself DST-adjusted
# by some external mechanism twice a year, this 22:02 UTC control-arm
# schedule needs the identical adjustment to keep the same 47-minute/
# non-multiple-of-5 relationship. ACTION ITEM, unchanged from v2: once
# SSH access works, read the live crontab's actual text/mechanism and
# either mirror it here or confirm it is unnecessary.
#
# No --enable-equity-orders / --enable-crypto-orders flags: this stays
# a read-only signal-generation + paper-position-tracking run until the
# user explicitly approves enabling real paper order submission.
#
# Guard-file isolation (unchanged from v2): AI_STOCK_RADAR_GUARD_DIR
# below points HIGH_WATER_MARK_PATH (src/live/position_state.py) at a
# control-arm-only directory -- without this it would default to
# $HOME/.ai_stock_radar_guard/ and silently collide with the live
# deployment's own guard file, since both run as root on the same
# server. This is the path already used in the deploy commands reported
# to the user (/root/AI-Stock-Radar-Control/.guard).

AI_STOCK_RADAR_GUARD_DIR=/root/AI-Stock-Radar-Control/.guard
02 22 * * 1-5 cd /root/AI-Stock-Radar-Control && /root/AI-Stock-Radar-Control/.venv/bin/python3 scripts/run_control_arm_decision.py --state-path /root/AI-Stock-Radar-Control/data/live/position_state.json --decision-log-directory /root/AI-Stock-Radar-Control/data/live/decisions --env-file /root/AI-Stock-Radar-Control/.env >> /root/AI-Stock-Radar-Control/logs/daily_decision.log 2>&1
